Answer the sRGB variants by name, and print the name when one is still unknown.

cocotron 0148 traced the NULL colour space in iA Writer back to IFColorSampleColor, which passes its
own first argument through, so the NULL came from the application. This says where the application
got it.

O2ColorSpaceCreateWithName already printed "unknown color space name" and then CFShow(name), and the
CFShow output does not appear in the log at all: the message that there WAS an unknown name arrived
every time and the name never did. Printed on the same line as a C string it is one word:

    unknown color space name: kCGColorSpaceLinearSRGB

kO2ColorSpaceLinearSRGB, kO2ColorSpaceExtendedSRGB and kO2ColorSpaceExtendedLinearSRGB are all
declared at the top of this file and none of the three was ever answered, so asking for one got NULL.
They join the RGB branch with the other three. The gamma and the extended range are still not
modelled, which is the same honest limitation the greys above already carry.

MEASURED on the Editor preferences pane, the path 0139 and 0148 were driven on:

    before   60 CIDER_CSNULL lines, 1 unknown color space name
    after     0 CIDER_CSNULL lines, 0 unknown color space names

AND THE RENDERING DOES NOT CHANGE, which is worth saying because I expected it to. The six highlight
colour swatches are the colours that needed a colour space at all, and the capture of that pane is
BYTE IDENTICAL across the two builds, 46360 bytes either way. A colour built on a NULL space kept
its components and drew correctly regardless; what this closes is a NULL that three guards were
holding up, not a visible defect.
--- a/Onyx2D/O2ColorSpace.m
+++ b/Onyx2D/O2ColorSpace.m
@@ -18,6 +18,7 @@
 CONNECTION WITH THE SOFTWARE OR THE USE OR OTHER DEALINGS IN THE SOFTWARE. */
 
 #import <CoreFoundation/CFBase.h>
+#import <CoreFoundation/CFString.h>
 #import <Foundation/NSString.h>
 #import <Onyx2D/O2ColorSpace.h>
 #include <dlfcn.h>
@@ -168,8 +169,16 @@
         cs->_name = (CFStringRef) [(id) name copy];
         return cs;
     }
+    /* THE sRGB VARIANTS TOO. All four are declared above and none of them was answered here, so
+     * asking for one got NULL: iA Writer asks for kCGColorSpaceLinearSRGB opening the Editor
+     * preferences pane, and that NULL travelled through colorWithSample:colorSpace: into
+     * O2ColorCreate. The gamma and the extended range are still not modelled, which is the same
+     * honest limitation as the greys above. */
     if (CFStringCompare(name, kO2ColorSpaceGenericRGB, 0) == kCFCompareEqualTo ||
         CFStringCompare(name, kO2ColorSpaceGenericRGBLinear, 0) == kCFCompareEqualTo ||
+        CFStringCompare(name, kO2ColorSpaceLinearSRGB, 0) == kCFCompareEqualTo ||
+        CFStringCompare(name, kO2ColorSpaceExtendedSRGB, 0) == kCFCompareEqualTo ||
+        CFStringCompare(name, kO2ColorSpaceExtendedLinearSRGB, 0) == kCFCompareEqualTo ||
         CFStringCompare(name, kO2ColorSpaceAdobeRGB1998, 0) == kCFCompareEqualTo) {
         O2ColorSpaceRef cs = [[O2ColorSpace alloc] initWithDeviceRGB];
         cs->_name = (CFStringRef) [(id) name copy];
@@ -181,8 +190,15 @@
         return cs;
     }
 
-    printf("unknown color space name\n");
-    CFShow(name);
+    /* THE NAME ON THE SAME LINE. CFShow printed nothing at all here, so the one unknown name in an
+     * iA Writer run stayed unnamed while the message that there WAS one arrived every time. */
+    {
+        char buf[128];
+        if (!CFStringGetCString(name, buf, sizeof(buf), kCFStringEncodingUTF8))
+            buf[0] = '\0';
+        fprintf(stderr, "unknown color space name: %s\n", buf);
+        fflush(stderr);
+    }
     return NULL;
 }
 
